          "turn_index": 0,
          "type": "turn",
          "question": "I stopped my blood pressure tablets two months ago and my home readings are now around 155/95. Is that an emergency, and what does guidance describe about treatment and follow-up?",
          "answer": "Your blood pressure readings of 155/95 mmHg are elevated, and you should talk to a doctor or other healthcare professional soon for an assessment (research-specialist). These readings are not typically considered a hypertensive emergency, which usually involves much higher numbers along with signs of organ damage (research-specialist). However, it is important to have your blood pressure checked by a medical professional and discuss your medication (research-specialist). The red-flag table for emergencies did not match this description, though if symptoms are severe, worsening, or new and unexplained, medical advice should be sought (diagnostic-specialist). Hypertension is defined as persistently raised pressure in the arteries, sustained over time rather than on a single occasion (diagnostic-specialist).\n\nRegarding treatment, your blood pressure of 155/95 mmHg falls into a category where drug treatment, alongside lifestyle measures, is typically recommended according to major cardiology society consensus guidance (research-specialist). For blood pressure in this range, often categorized as Stage 2 hypertension, guidance usually recommends starting with two medications because one alone may not be enough (guideline-hypertension-first-line-treatment, research-specialist). Common first-line medications include thiazide-type diuretics, angiotensin-converting-enzyme (ACE) inhibitors, angiotensin receptor blockers (ARBs), and dihydropyridine calcium-channel blockers, though ACE inhibitors and ARBs are not used together (guideline-hypertension-first-line-treatment, research-specialist). For most people, a blood pressure target of below 130/80 mmHg is recommended (guideline-coronary-artery-disease-secondary-prevention, research-specialist).\n\nAfter starting or changing blood pressure medication, guidance recommends reassessing blood pressure at roughly monthly intervals until the target blood pressure is reached (guideline-hypertension-diagnosis-and-bp-targets, guideline-hypertension-first-line-treatment, research-specialist; Major cardiology society consensus guidance (educational summary), \"Hypertension: diagnostic thresholds and blood-pressure targets\", diagnostic-specialist). Once blood pressure is stable at the target, reviews are typically recommended every three to six months (guideline-hypertension-diagnosis-and-bp-targets, guideline-hypertension-first-line-treatment, research-specialist; Major cardiology society consensus guidance (educational summary), \"Hypertension: diagnostic thresholds and blood-pressure targets\", diagnostic-specialist). Monthly reviews may also include checking medication adherence, kidney function, and potassium levels, especially after starting certain types of blood pressure medications (guideline-hypertension-diagnosis-and-bp-targets, guideline-hypertension-first-line-treatment, research-specialist).\n\nIt is worth noting that diagnostic thresholds for hypertension can differ; United States guidance describes hypertension as beginning at 130/80 mmHg, whereas European guidance defines office hypertension as starting at 140/90 mmHg (guideline-hypertension-diagnosis-and-bp-targets, research-specialist).\n\nNot medical advice. This is an educational software project. It does not diagnose or treat, and it must not be used for real medical decisions.",
